    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.events.snoozes">Snoozed alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.snoozed">Snoozed right now</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.snoozed.Desc">Alerts you snoozed for one character, from a toast or here. End one to get it again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Snoozes.None">Nothing is snoozed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Snoozes.End">End</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Snoozes.EndFor">End the snooze of {0} for {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Snoozes.EndAll">End all</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.keepSnoozes">Keep snoozes when MultiPreview restarts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.keepSnoozes.Desc">Off: every snooze ends when MultiPreview closes. On: a 15-minute or 1-hour snooze keeps running after a restart.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.snoozes.Title">Snoozing one alert for one character</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.snoozes.Body">Right-click a toast to snooze that alert for that character: for 15 minutes, for 1 hour, or until MultiPreview restarts. The menu closes by itself when the pointer moves away from it. The same alert still comes for your other characters, and that character's other alerts still come.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A snoozed alert shows no border, badge or toast and plays no sound. The alert history still lists it, marked Snoozed.&#x0A;- **Snoozed right now** lists what is snoozed, and a thumbnail's right-click menu lists that character's. Either place ends a snooze.&#x0A;- To silence every alert of a character, use Mute alerts in the thumbnail's menu instead.&#x0A;- **Keep snoozes when MultiPreview restarts** is off: closing MultiPreview ends every snooze. Turned on, the timed snoozes are kept in alert_snoozes.json in your settings folder and run out their time.&#x0A;&#x0A;A snooze can also be started here, without a toast: in **Snoozed right now**, choose a character (or type a name), an alert and a length, and press **Add snooze**; Mute alerts in a thumbnail's menu works without toasts too.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A snooze is kept by the character's name: if the character is renamed, the snooze matches nothing and stays until it runs out or MultiPreview restarts; end it from **Snoozed right now**. Up to 200 snoozes are kept.&#x0A;- Timed snoozes follow this PC's clock: set forward, they end early; set back, they last longer, and one with more than an hour and a minute left counts as over.&#x0A;- A snooze file with an entry more than an hour and a minute ahead is not read at all, and the error log says so (after a clock set back it can be your own file). In a settings folder that is a link, snoozes are not kept across a restart; the error log says so once, not at every change.&#x0A;- An entry whose character or alert holds a control character is left out. With a read-only snooze file, a snooze you ended comes back after a restart.&#x0A;- A toast and its menu work with the mouse only; Esc goes to EVE, not to the menu.&#x0A;- Adding a snooze here does not put back a thumbnail that was enlarged for that alert; it goes back at its own end.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.sounds.dnd">Do not disturb</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.dndHotkey">Do not disturb on or off</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.dndHotkey.Desc">Mutes alert sounds and spoken alerts, and holds alerts sent to Discord, until you press it again. Borders, badges and toasts go on.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.dndCritical">Critical alerts still play</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.dndCritical.Desc">With do not disturb on, a critical alert still plays its sound, is still spoken and still goes to Discord.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.quietHours">Quiet hours every day</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.quietHours.Desc">Do not disturb turns itself on and off by this PC's clock.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.quietFrom">Quiet from</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.quietTo">Quiet until</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.quietTo.Desc">A time such as 07:00. A span over midnight works.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Err.Time">Enter a time of day such as 23:00 or 7:30.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Dnd.On">Do not disturb: on. Alert sounds and spoken alerts are muted, and alerts sent to Discord are held.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Dnd.Off">Do not disturb: off</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.Dnd">Do not disturb</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.DndOn">Do not disturb: on</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.dnd.Title">Do not disturb</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.dnd.Body">Do not disturb holds the alert sounds, spoken alerts and alerts sent to Discord, and nothing else: borders, badges and toasts still show, Windows notifications still come and a thumbnail still grows. Use it on voice comms or while you stream.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Do not disturb on or off** switches it on and off, and so does the tray menu. A note near the cursor says which. While it is on, the tray icon's tip says so, and so does a line on this page.&#x0A;- **Critical alerts still play** is on at first: a critical alert still plays, and is still spoken. Turn it off for complete silence.&#x0A;- **Quiet hours every day** turns it on and off by the clock every day. **Quiet from** 23:00 and **Quiet until** 07:00 is one night. With the same time in **Quiet from** and **Quiet until** there are no quiet hours.&#x0A;- Pressing the hotkey during quiet hours gives the sounds back until the quiet hours end. Pressing it outside them mutes until you press again, or until the next quiet hours end.&#x0A;- Restarting MultiPreview forgets the hotkey's state. The quiet hours are a setting and stay.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.sounds.escalation">Repeating a critical alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.escalate">Repeat a critical alert you have not looked at</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.escalate.Desc">While that client has not come to the front, its sound plays again, louder each time. It needs Play alert sounds switched on.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.escalateAfter">Repeat after</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.escalateAfter.Desc">Seconds from the alert, and from one repeat to the next.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.escalateRepeats">How many times</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.escalateRepeats.Desc">After the last repeat MultiPreview plays no more sound until you have looked. With spoken alerts on, the alert is then spoken once more.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.escalation.Title">Repeating a critical alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.escalation.Body">A single sound is easy to miss under voice comms. With **Repeat a critical alert you have not looked at** on, a critical alert whose client you have not brought to the front plays its sound again after **Repeat after**, and again, up to **How many times** times. Each repeat is louder by a quarter of the scale, up to full volume.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A repeat is the alert's sound played again: **Play alert sounds** on this page must be on, and on a new install it is off. With it off, nothing is repeated.&#x0A;- Looking at the client ends it at once. So does closing the client, muting the character, snoozing that alert or switching its event off.&#x0A;- Several alerts of one kind on one client are repeated as one.&#x0A;- When several clients are due at the same moment, their repeats come one a second.&#x0A;- An event's own sound quiet time on this page is kept: a repeat waits for it.&#x0A;- With do not disturb on and **Critical alerts still play** on, repeats still come but not louder. With that switch off, nothing is repeated until do not disturb ends.&#x0A;- Warning and info alerts are never repeated.&#x0A;- Each repeat also flashes the client's border again, as the alert did. A client in front does not flash, nor when **Alert border width** (Alerts › Look &amp; severity) is 0 or while do not disturb holds the sounds. With **Play alert sounds** off there is no sound to repeat, but the border still flashes.&#x0A;- No single action stops the repeats on every client, except switching off **Repeat a critical alert you have not looked at**.&#x0A;- The most that can come is clients times critical events times **How many times**, at one sound a second; long sound files overlap.&#x0A;- A repeat counts as a sound for the event's quiet time: when that is longer than **Repeat after**, the repeats come that far apart.&#x0A;- With do not disturb on and **Critical alerts still play** off, the repeats wait; when do not disturb ends, every critical alert still unseen starts its repeats, one sound a second.&#x0A;- A run of repeats that is over does not start again for the same unseen alerts; looking at the client resets it.&#x0A;- If the once-a-second check behind the repeats fails, the error log gets one entry, not one a second, until a check goes through.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.straggler">Left behind</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.stragglerWait">Left behind: wait after the last jump</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.stragglerWait.Desc">When most of a group has jumped to another system and nothing has moved for this long, each member that did not make the jump gets the alert. A member that jumps into the system they left within 5 minutes of them and then stays put there for 60 seconds while most of the others are still away gets it as well (after this wait, where it is longer); a character that logs in there does not. Raise it if a client that only loads slowly gets flagged. Used only while Left behind is on.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.straggler.Title">Left behind: a group member that missed the jump</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.straggler.Body">MultiPreview knows which system each character is in. With **Left behind** switched on in the event table, it watches your groups (Clients › Groups) for a member that misses a jump.&#x0A;&#x0A;- When more members of a group jump from one system to another than stay behind, each one that did not make the jump gets the alert: the one still in the old system, and the one that took another gate. A member that came into that system only after the others had left is not left behind, and one that jumped and is back when the wait is over does not count as gone. A member that jumps into that system within 5 minutes of the others leaving it and then stays put there for 60 seconds while most of them are still away gets the alert as well (after the wait, where that is longer); a character that logs in there does not.&#x0A;- The toast says where the others went and how many of them: Jita → Perimeter 3/4. To see it, toasts must be on for **Warning** alerts (Alerts › Look &amp; severity); the alert history has that line too.&#x0A;- Nothing is said while the jumps are still coming in. **Left behind: wait after the last jump** is how long it must have been still. Raise it if a client that only loads slowly gets the alert, as in time dilation.&#x0A;- One scout going ahead is not a jump wave, and neither is half the group. A group of two never raises it.&#x0A;- It is off until you switch it on. A character you leave somewhere on purpose can be snoozed from its toast.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Clients that jump further apart than the wait are each a wave of one, and a wave of one tells no one.&#x0A;- A character is told once each time the others leave. One you left behind on purpose is told again at every departure: keep it out of the group, snooze the event for it, or give its group its own choice of this event under **Alerts for one group** (Alerts › Events).&#x0A;- A scout that trails the group on purpose is told at every stop of the others that lasts a minute. A slow ship that needs more than a minute per system is told once per gate until it is 5 minutes behind, and after that not at all.&#x0A;- A pod, a clone jump, a wormhole and a filament all count as a jump into a system, and docking keeps a stay running.&#x0A;- It goes by this PC's clock: set back, it tells late and misses the departures in between; set forward, it can lose a wave. Clients in different game languages may spell a system differently, and a mixed group then never matches. After a change of the log folder every system is told again, which seldom matters because a system a character is already in counts as no move.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.refused">Keys Windows refused</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.refused.Desc">Windows gives a key combination to one program at a time. A hotkey another program took first does nothing here.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.NotYet">Not checked yet. Hotkeys are set while an EVE client is running and hotkeys are not paused.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.None">Windows gave MultiPreview every hotkey.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.Count">{0} hotkey(s) refused. Free the key in the other program, or pick a different one here.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.Taken">Another program has it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.Error">Windows error {0}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.Running">Running now, and known to take hotkeys: {0}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.Usual">Programs that often take hotkeys: the NVIDIA overlay, AMD Software, ShareX, Greenshot, AutoHotkey, PowerToys.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.KeysRefused">Windows refused these hotkeys: {0}. Another program may have them. Click for details.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.refused.Title">Keys Windows refused</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.refused.Body">Windows gives each key combination to one program at a time. If another program took a hotkey first, pressing it does nothing in MultiPreview, and nothing tells you why.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Keys Windows refused** lists each such key with what it is bound to. Click one to change it.&#x0A;- The usual owners are overlays, screenshot tools and key remappers: the NVIDIA overlay, AMD Software, ShareX, Greenshot, AutoHotkey, PowerToys. The card names the ones that are running. For a hotkey with the Win key in it, the card also says that Windows keeps many Win combinations for itself.&#x0A;- MultiPreview also says so in a tray notification, once for each key.&#x0A;- Keys are checked whenever they are set: while an EVE client is running and hotkeys are not paused, and after you change a setting. **Check again** asks Windows once more, for when you have freed a key in the other program. Mouse buttons are never refused.&#x0A;- With **Hotkeys work while Ctrl, Shift or Alt is held** on, each key is also asked for with every mix of Ctrl, Alt, Shift and Win held. Windows keeps many of those for itself. Only the combination you chose is listed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Switch.LastAlertHere">The last alert is on this client</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.quietFrom.Desc">24-hour clock, such as 23:00.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.DndState.On">Do not disturb is on now. Alert sounds and spoken alerts are muted, and alerts sent to Discord are held.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.DndState.OnCritical">Do not disturb is on now. Alert sounds and spoken alerts are muted and alerts sent to Discord are held, but critical alerts still play, are spoken and go to Discord.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.Again">Check again</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Refused.WinKey">Windows keeps many combinations with the Win key for itself. A refused hotkey with Win in it is most likely one of them: pick a different combination.</sys:String>
