    <sys:String x:Key="L.Notify.Snooze">Snooze for 15 minutes</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.hub.windows.Title">Windows notifications</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.hub.windows.Body">With **Show alerts as Windows notifications** on, an alert also appears as a notification of Windows itself, with the character's name and the event; a Left behind alert also shows where the others went. It stays in Windows' Notification Center, so you can see what happened while you were away from the PC.&#x0A;&#x0A;- The first button brings that client to the front, and so does a click on the notification. The second button snoozes that alert for that character for 15 minutes.&#x0A;- There is one notification for each character and event. A newer alert updates it and counts up, and it pops up again at most every 30 seconds.&#x0A;- An alert on the client in front gets no notification, and a client's notifications go away when you look at it. A muted character and a snoozed alert get none.&#x0A;- The notification is silent: MultiPreview plays its own alert sounds. MultiPreview's do not disturb holds the alert sounds and the voice; a notification still comes.&#x0A;- Windows has its own switch for notifications: in Windows' settings under System, then Notifications, for all programs and for EVE MultiPreview alone. While that is off nothing appears, and a line on the **Test notification** card says so. **Show a test notification** shows one right away, so you can see for yourself; it has no buttons and goes away by itself after a minute.&#x0A;- Windows decides whether a notification pops up: with its own do not disturb on, or while a game runs in full screen, it goes to the Notification Center only. Windows keeps the newest 20, each for up to 12 hours after its last alert.&#x0A;- Windows can show a notification's text on the lock screen. If nobody should read there which character was attacked or where the others went, turn that off in Windows' notification settings.&#x0A;- While this is on, MultiPreview keeps one entry in the Windows registry and one picture file in its settings folder, so that Windows knows the name and the icon to show. Switching it off removes the entry and MultiPreview's notifications. The notifications are also taken away when MultiPreview closes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlarge">Enlarge the thumbnail on an alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlarge.Desc">The thumbnail grows for a few seconds, so you see what is happening there without switching.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeFrom">Enlarge for which alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeFrom.Desc">By severity: every event is critical, warning or info.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargePercent">Enlarged size</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargePercent.Desc">In percent of the thumbnail's own size. It is never larger than the monitor.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeSeconds">Stays enlarged for</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.look.enlargeSeconds.Desc">Seconds from the alert. It goes back at once when you switch to that client.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.look.enlarge.Title">Enlarging on an alert</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.look.enlarge.Body">With **Enlarge the thumbnail on an alert** on, the thumbnail of a client that raises an alert grows for a few seconds, so you can see what is happening there without switching to it.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Enlarge for which alerts** chooses by severity. **Enlarged size** and **Stays enlarged for** set how large and how long. A newer alert on that client keeps it enlarged longer.&#x0A;- It goes back by itself when the time is over, and at once when that client comes to the front, when you mute that character's alerts in the thumbnail's menu or snooze one of its alerts, or when its character logs off.&#x0A;- It grows around its own middle, never larger than the monitor, and is moved back onto the screen where it would stick out. Your layout is not changed, and nothing of the enlargement is saved.&#x0A;- The part that grew does not take clicks: to switch to that client, click where the thumbnail usually is. A click on the part that grew goes to whatever window of MultiPreview lies under it (another thumbnail, a second thumbnail, a stats overlay window, a crop window), and to nothing otherwise, so a thumbnail that grows under the pointer never switches clients by accident.&#x0A;- It stands above the thumbnails beside it only while thumbnails are kept on top: with **Always on top**, or with **Keep above EVE clients** while an EVE client is in front. Otherwise it grows where it stands among the other windows.&#x0A;- It shows the thumbnail's picture, larger. For a minimised client, and with **Still thumbnails**, that is the last snapshot, not what is happening at this moment.&#x0A;- A thumbnail does not start to grow while the pointer is on it; one that is already enlarged stays so when the pointer arrives. One you are dragging and the client in front are not enlarged. While a menu of the app is open (a thumbnail's right-click menu, the tray menu or a toast's menu) no thumbnail is enlarged: an enlarged one goes back, and grows again when the menu has closed. Second thumbnails and crop windows never are.&#x0A;- Do not disturb holds the alert sounds and the voice; the thumbnail still grows.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.events.groups">Alerts per group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.groups">Alerts for one group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.groups.Desc">A group can have its own choice of which events are on. An event left as everyone follows the table above.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Group">Group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.AsEveryoneOn">As everyone (on)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.AsEveryoneOff">As everyone (off)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.On">On for this group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Off">Off for this group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.ChoiceFor">{0} for the group {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Members">Members: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.NoMembers">This group has no members in this profile, so its choices do nothing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Overridden">Members who are in another group as well do not all get what the rows above say. When a character's groups disagree about an event, on wins: the event is on for that character. A group that left the event as everyone has no say.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.NoGroups">There are no groups yet. Make one on the Groups page.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Clear">Clear this group's choices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.groups.Title">Alerts per group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.groups.Body">A group (Clients › Groups) can have its own choice of which events are on, so that the miners are told of a full cargo hold and the combat pilots are not.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Pick the group, then set an event to on or off for it. An event left as everyone follows its switch in **Alert events** above.&#x0A;- A group can switch on an event that is off for everyone, and switch off one that is on.&#x0A;- A character in no group always has the switches for everyone. For **Left behind**, the groups of the member who is left behind decide.&#x0A;- For a character in several groups, on wins: when its groups disagree about an event, the event is on. A group that left the event as everyone has no say. Under its rows, the card lists each member who gets something other than the rows say, with a group that says so.&#x0A;- An event that is off for a character is no alert for it: nothing is shown, played or spoken, and it is not in the alert history.&#x0A;- A custom rule can be switched off for a group. A rule that is switched off on its own page is off for everybody.&#x0A;- A group's choices go with it when you rename it, and do nothing while it has no members.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.neutralized">Capacitor neutralized</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.jammed">Jammed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.dps_high">Incoming DPS high</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.reps_stopped">Reps stopped</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.ewarNote">Capacitor neutralized and Jammed are off until you switch them on, and are read from an English game client only.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.ewar.Title">Capacitor neutralized and Jammed</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.ewar.Body">Two events for what often comes before the damage: **Capacitor neutralized**, when an energy neutralizer takes capacitor from your ship, and **Jammed**, when your ship is jammed and loses its target locks.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Both are off until you switch them on in the table above.&#x0A;- Each is raised once when it starts, then at most every 30 seconds for that character while it goes on.&#x0A;- They are read from the wording of an English game client. With the game in another language they raise nothing.&#x0A;- With **PVE mode** on, an NPC that neutralizes or jams you raises nothing, and neither does a player whose log line shows no corporation or alliance ticker.&#x0A;- A nosferatu that drains your capacitor is not covered, only a neutralizer.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Speech.TryFailed">Windows could not say that. Check that a voice is installed and that a sound device is connected.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.notifyTest">Test notification</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.notifyTest.Desc">Shows one notification right away, so you can see whether Windows lets them through. It has no buttons and goes away by itself after a minute.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Notify.TestButton">Show a test notification</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Notify.Blocked">Windows is not showing MultiPreview's notifications: they are turned off in Windows' own settings, for all programs or for EVE MultiPreview, or your organisation's settings block them. Where you can, turn them on there (System, then Notifications).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Notify.TestLine">This is a test. It goes away by itself after a minute.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.GetsOn">{0}: {1} is on ({2} says on)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.GetsOff">{0}: {1} is off ({2} says off)</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Join">{0}, {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.SharedName">More than one group has this name. They share these choices, and their members are listed together.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.ClearAsk">Clear the alert choices of the group “{0}”? Every event follows the switch for everyone again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.ClearYes">Clear the choices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.ClearNo">Keep the choices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.OpenGroups">Open the Groups page</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.NotKept">That choice was not kept: there is no room for more group choices. Clear choices another group no longer needs, or give this group a shorter name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupAlerts.Note">Own alert choices for {0}: see Alerts for one group, on Alerts › Events.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Notify.TestFailed">The test notification could not be shown. error_log.txt in the logs folder may say why.</sys:String>
    <!-- Ideas batch 7 (Plan 11) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.sounds.groups">Sounds per group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.groups">Sounds for one group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.sounds.groups.Desc">A group from Clients › Groups can play its own sound: one for every event, or one for a single event. Without one, the event's own sound plays.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupSounds.Group">Group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupSounds.Every">Every event</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupSounds.UsesEvent">The event's own sound</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupSounds.NoGroups">No groups yet. Make one on Clients › Groups.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupSounds.Members">Members: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.GroupSounds.Overlap">{0} is also in {1}, which comes first on the Groups page. Where both have a sound for the same event, or both for every event, {1}'s plays.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.groups.Title">Sounds per group</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.sounds.groups.Body">Give a group of clients its own sound, so you hear which crew needs you before you look: one tone for the miners, another for the combat pilots.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Every event** plays for each alert of the group's members.&#x0A;- A sound for **one event** plays instead of that, for that event.&#x0A;- An event the group has no sound for plays its own sound (the table above), as before.&#x0A;&#x0A;A character in two groups: a sound for that event beats a sound for every event, and otherwise the group that comes first on the Groups page plays. The card names who that is. Alert sounds must be on (**Play alert sounds**), and do not disturb holds a group's sound as any other. A sound file that is no longer there plays the event's own sound instead.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Unit.Dps">DPS</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.dpsThreshold">Incoming DPS high: above</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.dpsThreshold.Desc">The damage per second a character must take, on average, for the alert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.dpsSeconds">Incoming DPS high: for</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.dpsSeconds.Desc">How long the damage must stay that high before the alert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.repsSeconds">Reps stopped: after</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.repsSeconds.Desc">How long after the last remote repair a hit raises the alert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.combatNote">Incoming DPS high and Reps stopped are off until you turn them on in the table above. Both count NPC damage, also in PvE mode.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.combatWatch.Title">Incoming DPS high and Reps stopped</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.combatWatch.Body">Two events for a fight that is turning against you. Both are off until you turn them on in the event table, and both count damage from NPCs too, also in **PvE mode**: they are meant for ratting, where the ordinary Under attack is silent.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Incoming DPS high**: the damage a character takes stays above **Incoming DPS high: above** for **Incoming DPS high: for** (400 DPS for 10 seconds unless you change them). A pause in the fire longer than that time starts over.&#x0A;- **Reps stopped**: a character was being repaired by another ship (armor or shield; capacitor transfers don't count) and takes a hit **Reps stopped: after** seconds after the last repair. It is said once, until the next repair comes. Reps that stopped more than a minute before that are taken for another fight.&#x0A;&#x0A;Both are critical. After an alert the event waits 30 seconds for that character, or its quiet time where that is longer. They read the hit's colour and the repair lines that Logi stats read, so they work with the game in any language.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.alerts.hub.push">Phone and Discord</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.push">Send alerts to Discord</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.push.Desc">An alert is also posted to a Discord channel of yours, and the Discord app on your phone shows it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.pushFrom">Send which alerts</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.pushFrom.Desc">By severity: every event is critical, warning or info.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.pushAway">Only while you're away from the PC</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.pushAway.Desc">Sends nothing while Windows saw your keyboard or mouse in the last 2 minutes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Push.Test">Test message from EVE MultiPreview: alerts will arrive here.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Push.More">…and {0} more</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.hub.push.Title">Phone and Discord</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.hub.push.Body">Get your alerts on your phone while the clients run in another room. MultiPreview posts them to a **Discord webhook**: a link that lets a program post to one channel of a server.&#x0A;&#x0A;- In Discord, in a server of your own: **Server Settings › Integrations › Webhooks › New Webhook**, pick the channel, then **Copy Webhook URL**.&#x0A;- Paste the link into **Discord webhook** here and press **Save link**, then **Send a test message**.&#x0A;- Turn on notifications for that channel in the Discord app on your phone.&#x0A;&#x0A;A message names the character and the event, nothing else. Critical alerts only unless you choose more; and with **Only while you're away from the PC** on, nothing is sent while you are using the PC. Do not disturb holds these as it holds the sounds. Alerts are sent at most every 2 seconds, gathered into one message, and the same alert of one character not again within a minute.&#x0A;&#x0A;**Treat the link like a password**: anyone who has it can post to that channel. MultiPreview keeps it encrypted for your Windows user in its settings file, never shows it whole again and never writes it to a log; on another PC or for another Windows user you paste it again. If Discord says the webhook no longer exists, MultiPreview stops sending until you save a new link.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.pushWebhook">Discord webhook</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.hub.pushWebhook.Desc">The link Discord gave you for the channel. It is kept encrypted and never shown whole again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.LinkBox">Discord webhook link</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Save">Save link</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Remove">Remove link</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.RemoveTitle">Remove the Discord link?</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.RemoveBody">MultiPreview forgets the link and sends nothing more. The webhook itself stays in Discord until you delete it there.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.TestButton">Send a test message</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.NotAWebhook">That is not a Discord webhook link. It looks like https://discord.com/api/webhooks/…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.NoLink">No link yet. In Discord: Server Settings › Integrations › Webhooks › New Webhook › Copy Webhook URL, then paste it here.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Unreadable">The saved link can't be read here: it was saved on another PC or by another Windows user. Paste it again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Status.Sent">The last message reached Discord at {0:t}.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Status.Gone">Discord says this webhook doesn't exist. Nothing is sent until you save a new link.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Status.Refused">Discord refused the last message ({0}).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Status.Unreachable">Discord could not be reached. MultiPreview keeps trying for 5 minutes per alert.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Push.Status.Limited">Discord asked MultiPreview to slow down. Messages wait their turn.</sys:String>
    <!-- Ideas batch 8 (Plan 12) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.app.general.session">While you play</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.general.keepAwake">Keep the PC awake while EVE runs</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.general.keepAwake.Desc">While a character is logged in, Windows does not go to sleep. The screen can still turn off.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.keepAwake.Title">Keeping the PC awake</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.keepAwake.Body">A mining or industry session ends when Windows puts the PC to sleep because nobody touched the mouse. With **Keep the PC awake while EVE runs** on, MultiPreview asks Windows not to sleep while at least one client has a character logged in.&#x0A;&#x0A;- The screen still turns off on its own timer.&#x0A;- A client at the login screen does not count.&#x0A;- When the last character's client closes, when you turn this off, or when MultiPreview closes, Windows may sleep again. If MultiPreview stops unexpectedly, Windows lets go of the request by itself.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.client_memory">Client memory high</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Events.Group.client">Clients</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Unit.Mb">MB</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Unit.Min">min</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.memoryLimit">Client memory high: above</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.memoryLimit.Desc">The memory a client may use before the event is raised. Used while Client memory high is on in the table above.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.memoryGrowth">Client memory high: grows by</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.memoryGrowth.Desc">How much a client's memory may grow within the time below before the event is raised.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.memoryMinutes">Client memory high: within</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.memoryMinutes.Desc">The time the growth is measured over.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.memory.Title">Client memory high</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.memory.Body">A long EVE session grows in memory until the client stutters or closes. **Client memory high** tells you early, so you can relog when it suits you. Turn it on in the event table (under Clients); it is off until you do.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Above:** a client that uses this much memory is told once, and again only after its memory came well below it or after a relog.&#x0A;- **Grows by** and **within:** a client whose memory grew this much over this time is told, and then the time is counted again. The first ten minutes of a client are not counted: a client loads a lot right after it starts.&#x0A;&#x0A;MultiPreview reads each client's memory every few seconds, as the process stats on the thumbnails do. The memory of the graphics card is not counted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Sheet.Title">Hotkeys of this profile</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Sheet.None">No hotkeys are set. Set them on Clients › Hotkeys &amp; switching.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Sheet.More">and {0} more on Clients › Hotkeys &amp; switching</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Sheet.Close">Click to close</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.sheet">Show the hotkey cheat sheet</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.hotkeys.sheet.Desc">Shows every hotkey of this profile over the thumbnails. Press again or click it to close.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.HotkeySheet">Hotkey cheat sheet</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.sheet.Title">The hotkey cheat sheet</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.hotkeys.sheet.Body">With several profiles, each with keys per character, per group and for the app, it is easy to forget which key does what. The cheat sheet shows every hotkey of the current profile, by the name its card has here, over the thumbnails.&#x0A;&#x0A;- Open it with **Show the hotkey cheat sheet** or from the tray menu (**Hotkey cheat sheet**).&#x0A;- It closes when you press the key again, click it, or after 30 seconds.&#x0A;- It never takes the focus from EVE, so your keys keep going to the game while it shows.&#x0A;&#x0A;While hotkeys are paused, its key does nothing either.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.general.downtime">Downtime countdown and warnings</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.general.downtime.Desc">The tray menu counts down to the daily downtime at 11:00 UTC, and a tray note warns you 15 and 5 minutes before it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.DowntimeHours">Downtime (11:00 UTC) in {0} h {1} min</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.DowntimeMinutes">Downtime (11:00 UTC) in {0} min</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Tray.DowntimeWarning">Downtime (11:00 UTC) in {0} min. Logged in: {1}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.downtime.Title">Downtime</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.downtime.Body">EVE's server goes down every day at 11:00 UTC. With **Downtime countdown and warnings** on:&#x0A;&#x0A;- the first line of the tray menu counts down to it;&#x0A;- a tray note 15 minutes and again 5 minutes before it names every character that is logged in, so a ratting or mining fleet can dock in time.&#x0A;&#x0A;EVE's logs say when a character undocks but never when it docks, so the note names everyone who is logged in, not only those in space. When MultiPreview starts, or the first character logs in, less than 15 minutes before downtime, the note comes at once.</sys:String>
    <!-- Ideas batch 9 (Plan 13) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.thumbs.text.clientState">When a client stops</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.thumbs.text.notResponding">Show “Not responding” on a frozen client</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.thumbs.text.notResponding.Desc">When Windows reports that a client's window has stopped answering, its thumbnail says so.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.thumbs.text.disconnected">Show “Disconnected” when a client loses the server</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.thumbs.text.disconnected.Desc">When a logged-in client has had no connection to EVE's server for the time below, its thumbnail says so.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.thumbs.text.disconnectSeconds">Disconnected after</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.thumbs.text.disconnectSeconds.Desc">How long a client must be without its server connection before it is marked.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.thumbs.text.clientState.Title">When a client stops</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.thumbs.text.clientState.Body">A frozen or disconnected client looks like an idle one until you switch to it. MultiPreview can mark its thumbnail:&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Not responding**: Windows says the client's window has stopped answering (the same test Task Manager uses: no answer for five seconds). The mark goes when the client answers again. Right-click the thumbnail and choose **End this frozen client…** to end it; MultiPreview asks first, and leaves it alone if it has started answering by then.&#x0A;- **Disconnected**: a logged-in client has had no connection to EVE's server for the time you set. MultiPreview asks Windows which connections each client has; it sends nothing anywhere.&#x0A;&#x0A;To stay clear of false marks, a client that never had a server connection, or whose connections go through a proxy on this PC, is never marked Disconnected, so some VPN and proxy setups show nothing. Both marks are off until you turn them on. The same mark shows on that client's second thumbnail and crop windows.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Thumb.NotResponding">Not responding</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Thumb.Disconnected">Disconnected</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.client_hung">Client not responding</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.client_closed">Client closed unexpectedly</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.clientNote">Client not responding and Client closed unexpectedly are off until you turn them on in the table above. The first comes after a client has been frozen for 30 seconds; the second when a client ends with an error, not when you quit it yourself.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.clientStops.Title">Clients that stop</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.clientStops.Body">**Client not responding** is raised when Windows has reported a client's window frozen for 30 seconds, once for each freeze. It needs a character logged in on that client.&#x0A;&#x0A;**Client closed unexpectedly** is raised when a client's process ends with an error code: a crash, or Task Manager ending it. Quitting EVE yourself ends the client without an error and raises nothing, and so does **Close client** in MultiPreview. The alert names the character the client had last; the error code is kept in the alert history.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A client running as administrator while MultiPreview is not can't be watched for this.&#x0A;- Both are critical alerts, so do-not-disturb lets their sound through unless you changed that.&#x0A;- A closed client has no window left, so its alert is not enlarged. It pops up, plays its sound, is pushed and goes into the history; it is spoken when spoken alerts are on, and shown as a Windows notification (without buttons) when those are on, both by the character's name.&#x0A;&#x0A;To see a frozen or disconnected client on its thumbnail, turn on the marks under Thumbnails › Text &amp; borders › When a client stops.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Ctx.EndFrozen">End this frozen client…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Msg.EndFrozenConfirm">{0}'s client is not responding. End it now? Anything it has not saved is lost.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Msg.EndFrozenDone">Ended {0}'s client.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Msg.EndFrozenRecovered">{0}'s client is responding again: nothing was ended.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Msg.EndFrozenGone">{0}'s client has already closed.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Msg.EndFrozenRefused">Windows did not let MultiPreview end {0}'s client.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.AlertEvt.char_idle">Character idle</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.idleMinutes">Character idle: after</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.alerts.events.idleMinutes.Desc">How long a character that was fighting, mining or repairing may go without it before the event is raised.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.idle.Title">Character idle</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.alerts.events.idle.Body">**Character idle** tells you a character has stopped working: the site is cleared, the rock is gone, the drones came home. It is raised when a character that was fighting, mining or repairing has written none of that to its game log for the time you set, and once until it starts again. Turn it on in the event table (under Clients); it is off until you do.&#x0A;&#x0A;- A character that has done none of it since it logged in (a trader, a hauler, a cyno alt) is never told.&#x0A;- A jump into another system or a fleet warp starts the count again, so moving to the next site is not idle. EVE does not log your own warps, so a warp longer than the time you set can raise it.&#x0A;- When **Miner stopped**, **Asteroid depleted** or **Cargo full** already told you of that character's stop, this event says nothing for it.&#x0A;- EVE's logs never say when a character docks, and an undock into the same system is not seen, so time spent docked counts toward the time you set: a character that docks after working is told once, and one that undocks again soon after may be told early.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.app.diagnostics.report">Bug report</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.diagnostics.bugReport">Create a bug report</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.diagnostics.bugReport.Desc">A zip with what a bug report needs: the version, Windows, your monitors, your settings and the end of each log. You see everything in it before you save it, and MultiPreview sends nothing.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Act.app.diagnostics.createReport">Create bug report…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Title">Bug report</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Intro">This is everything the zip will hold. Read it before you share it: names MultiPreview doesn't know, such as other players in your logs, are not hidden.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.MaskNames">Hide character names</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Note">Nothing is sent. The zip is saved where you choose, and you decide whom to give it to. Your Windows user name, the PC's name and your Discord webhook are always left out.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Save">Save as zip…</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Close">Close</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Saved">Saved as {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.Failed">Could not save the zip: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.BuildFailed">Could not make the report: {0}</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Report.ZipFilter">Zip files (*.zip)|*.zip</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Start.Crash.Report">Create a bug report</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.diagnostics.report.Title">Bug reports</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.diagnostics.report.Body">**Create bug report…** makes a zip you can attach to a bug report. It holds:&#x0A;&#x0A;- MultiPreview's version, the Windows version and build, the language and the look;&#x0A;- each monitor's size, place, work area and scaling;&#x0A;- your settings, with your Discord webhook left out;&#x0A;- the last 500 lines of each of MultiPreview's logs.&#x0A;&#x0A;Your Windows user name is taken out of every folder path, and the PC's name too. **Hide character names** (on until you turn it off) replaces your characters' names with Character 1, Character 2 and so on; names MultiPreview doesn't know, such as other players in a log, stay. You see every file before you save, and MultiPreview never sends the zip anywhere.</sys:String>
    <!-- Ideas batch 10 (Plan 14) -->
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Sec.clients.groups.focus">Focus mode</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.groups.focusKey">Focus mode on or off</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.groups.focusKey.Desc">Shows only the thumbnails of one group and hides the others, until you press it again.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.groups.focusGroup">Group to show</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.groups.focusGroup.Desc">The group of the client you are in follows you as you switch clients; a group you choose stays the same.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.Opt.FocusActiveGroup">The group of the client you are in</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.groups.focusAlerts">Show a hidden thumbnail while its alert is unseen</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.clients.groups.focusAlerts.Desc">A client outside the group that raises an alert shows until you look at it.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.groups.focus.Title">Focus mode</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.clients.groups.focus.Body">With many clients split by role, every thumbnail at once is a lot to look at. **Focus mode on or off** shows only the thumbnails of one group and hides the rest; press it again to show them all.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Group to show**: the group of the client you are in (it follows you as you switch clients), or one group you choose.&#x0A;- With no group to show (the client you are in is in no group or at character select, or the chosen group no longer exists), pressing the key leaves focus mode off and a note near the pointer says why; if you switch to such a client while it is on, every thumbnail shows.&#x0A;- **Show a hidden thumbnail while its alert is unseen** (on until you turn it off) lets a client outside the group show while it has an alert you have not looked at.&#x0A;- Focus mode hides the second thumbnails, crop windows and stats windows of the clients it hides too; while it is on, the **Focus mode** item in the tray menu is ticked and the tray tip says so, and a client that logs in shows for half a second. A thumbnail you hid another way stays hidden when focus mode ends.&#x0A;&#x0A;Focus mode is off again each time MultiPreview starts.</sys:String>
