    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.buttons.Title">Buttons for Stream Deck and friends</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.buttons.Body">**Commands for buttons** writes the command line for each command, one per running character, group and profile as well as the fixed ones.&#x0A;&#x0A;- **Copy command line** puts it on the clipboard: paste it into Touch Portal's run action, an AutoHotkey Run, or a Windows shortcut's Target.&#x0A;- **Save as shortcut…** makes a Windows shortcut. Stream Deck's Open action cannot pass arguments, so point it at that shortcut (or use the Advanced Launcher plugin with the command line).&#x0A;- **Try it** sends the command to this MultiPreview the way a button would, and tells you what came of it. Trying hide or show toggles it.&#x0A;&#x0A;A name with a quotation mark, or ending in a backslash, has no command line. If a button does nothing, run **Try it** first: it tells you whether MultiPreview took the command. When MultiPreview runs as administrator, **Try it** cannot show whether programs that are not run as administrator get through (see Commands from other programs).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.general.acceptCommandsUnelevated">Also take commands from programs that are not run as administrator</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.S.app.general.acceptCommandsUnelevated.Desc">Only matters when MultiPreview runs as administrator. Lets your other programs (a Stream Deck, a script) send commands without running as administrator too. Any ordinary program of your Windows account could then switch your clients and profiles, though it still cannot name a file or run anything. Off by default.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.commandsAdmin.Title">When MultiPreview runs as administrator</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="L.Help.app.general.commandsAdmin.Body">Windows keeps programs that are not run as administrator away from one that is, so a Stream Deck started the ordinary way gets no answer (exit code 3) from a MultiPreview run as administrator.&#x0A;&#x0A;You can run the sender as administrator too, or turn on **Also take commands from programs that are not run as administrator**. With it on, programs of your own Windows account that are not run as administrator can send the same commands; nothing is listened for on the network, and a command still never names a file.</sys:String>
